{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_WNcOj1v666AOPCKCoJW1M8R8","content":"1210\t+----+------------------------------------+------------------------------------+\n1211\t| #  | Construct (handler)                | Rule                               |\n1212\t+====+====================================+====================================+\n1213\t| 22 | script or request end              | (1) run                            |\n1214\t|    | (`shutdown_executor`,              | `register_shutdown_function`       |\n1215\t|    | `zend_call_destructors`)           | callbacks in registration order.   |\n1216\t|    |                                    | (2) **Destructor phase**: repeat { |\n1217\t|    |                                    | walk `$GLOBALS` in reverse         |\n1218\t|    |                                    | insertion order; for each entry    |\n1219\t|    |                                    | holding an object handle with rc   |\n1220\t|    |                                    | 1, remove the entry and `DELREF`   |\n1221\t|    |                                    | it, destroying it } until the      |\n1222\t|    |                                    | entry count is stable; then call   |\n1223\t|    |                                    | `__destruct` for every object      |\n1224\t|    |                                    | remaining in `finalizable` in      |\n1225\t|    |                                    | `:ord` order (pinned and unpinned  |\n1226\t|    |                                    | as above, not freed yet); scalars, |\n1227\t|    |                                    | strings and arrays in `$GLOBALS`   |\n1228\t|    |                                    | are untouched here, so destructors |\n1229\t|    |                                    | can read them. (3) Flush the       |\n1230\t|    |                                    | output buffers to the response     |\n1231\t|    |                                    | stream, nested unclosed buffers in |\n1232\t|    |                                    | stack order. (4) Release           |\n1233\t|    |                                    | `$GLOBALS` in reverse insertion    |\n1234\t|    |                                    | order, then the static tables,     |\n1235\t|    |                                    | then `FREE` every remaining object |\n1236\t|    |                                    | without calling destructors. An    |\n1237\t|    |                                    | uncaught `Throwable` runs this     |\n1238\t|    |                                    | sequence after the error output;   |\n1239\t|    |                                    | the VM's operational halts (budget |\n1240\t|    |                                    | exhaustion, cancellation) skip the |\n1241\t|    |                                    | destructor phase, as Zend skips it |\n1242\t|    |                                    | on fatal errors                    |\n1243\t+----+------------------------------------+------------------------------------+\n1244\t\n1245\t### 4.5 Oracle facts and anchor programs\n1246\t\n1247\tThree outputs were obtained first-hand from PHP 8.3 (image `php:8.3-cli`, host\n1248\tshiva, 2026-10-17) and are the fixture group `oracle-2026-10-17`, recorded\n1249\twith that provenance:\n1250\t\n1251\t- **F1** (copy lineage, rows 25 and 60): `$a=[10,20,30]; foreach ($a as &$v) {\n1252\t  echo \"$v,\"; if (first) { $b=$a; $a[2]=99; } }` prints `10,20,99,`.\n1253\t- **F2** (named arguments, row 57): with `function f($x) {}` and a `side()`\n1254\t  that echoes `late`, `f(bad: 1, x: side())` raises `Error: Unknown named\n1255\t  parameter $bad`, and `late` is never printed. `f(bad: side())` runs `side()`\n1256\t  and then raises the same `Error`.\n1257\t- **F3** (readonly clone, rows 66 and 67): with `public readonly int $x`\n1258\t  initialized to 1 in the constructor, `function __clone() { unset($this->x);\n1259\t  $this->x = 2; }` yields `2` with no error.\n1260\t\n1261\tThe oracle harness strips an uncaught-`Throwable` or plain-fatal block from\n1262\tthe captured stdout and reports it as the program's `:uncaught` result. The\n1263\tstrip ends at the block's own terminator (`thrown in FILE on line N` for an\n1264\tuncaught throwable, the `on line N` message end for a plain fatal), so output\n1265\twritten afterwards (shutdown functions and destructors of row 22) stays in\n1266\t`:out`.\n1267\t\n1268\tAnchor programs that every revision of the table has been traced against\n1269\t(expected PHP 8.3 output):"}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_7M9LJV0EDvqTFyF7taSeW02Z","content":"1900\tas data; its filesystem resolution is not (owner decision B8.7).\n1901\t\n1902\t### 11.3 Missing includes: two stages, two meanings\n1903\t\n1904\t1. **Publication**: every static `include`/`require` edge with a literal path\n1905\t   must be pinned to a concrete address. A target absent from the unit set and\n1906\t   from `published` is refused `:unpublished-import` by the lifted publisher\n1907\t   (`yang.antlr.md` 8.5.6: \"publication still requires a concrete address\").\n1908\t   There is **no** publication-time representation of an optional absent\n1909\t   include; that is the known-gaps ledger's open item\n1910\t   (\"publication-time-unavailable optional imports cannot be represented\n1911\t   without concrete segment pins\"), not solved here (owner decision B8.10).\n1912\t2. **Run time**: \"missing\" means the pinned content is unavailable or its\n1913\t   delivery is refused (`:absent` with `:address`, a mismatch, an untrusted\n1914\t   principal). `include` and `include_once` emit PHP's two warning records\n1915\t   and yield `false`; `require` and `require_once` emit the first warning and\n1916\t   throw `Error` with PHP's fatal text. The warning text retains the written\n1917\t   kind. A refused path is not added to the included-files dict, so a later\n1918\t   `_once` retries delivery. Each record carries the structured refusal under\n1919\t   `:yang.php/refusal`.\n1920\t\n1921\t## 12. Safepoints\n1922\t\n1923\t- `:loop` and `:call` hooks (`phpsp`) serve signals: `pcntl_signal` handlers\n1924\t  run at hooks via `stream/poll!` on a signal stream, and\n1925\t  `pcntl_async_signals` is the default and only mode. A recursion-depth bound\n1926\t  is available if a composition wants one (PHP has none; default off).\n1927\t- `max_execution_time` and `set_time_limit` are wall-clock in PHP and so\n1928\t  nondeterministic: the profile offers a deterministic **safepoint-count\n1929\t  budget** instead, raising the fatal with PHP's text, a recorded departure\n1930\t  (owner decision B8.5, corner C21).\n1931\t- **Statement ticks** (`declare(ticks=N)`, `register_tick_function`) need\n1932\t  `:line` sites before non-lambda statement nodes. `yang.safepoint` currently\n1933\t  refuses a selected site that is not a `:lambda` (`site-not-lambda`), and\n1934\t  `:line` insertion is safepoint slice 3, which Python tracing also needs.\n1935\t  Ticks therefore **depend on the shared `:line` extension**: P7 splits into\n1936\t  P7-a (signals, no dependency) and P7-b (ticks, behind safepoint slice 3).\n1937\t  Neither L1 to L4 nor this frontend extends `yang.safepoint`; the extension\n1938\t  is the safepoint seat's.\n1939\t- No thread switches: PHP is single-threaded, and one request is one task.\n1940\t\n1941\t## 13. Diagnostics\n1942\t\n1943\tCompile time: `:yang.php/syntax`; `:yang.php/unsupported {:construct}`;\n1944\t`:yang.php/not-php`; `:yang.php/unknown-builtin` (a warning);\n1945\t`:yang.php/compile-error` for PHP's compile-time fatals (a function redeclared\n1946\tin one unit, `break 0`, a duplicate parameter, `$this` reassignment, a\n1947\tpositional argument after a named one). Run time: guest warnings, notices and\n1948\tdeprecations as records on the guest-diagnostics stream; fatals as `Error`\n1949\tthrows; the uncaught-exception text pinned. Operational failures stay\n1950\toperational (`yang.antlr.md` section 11).\n1951\t\n1952\t## 14. The differential-corpus plan\n1953\t\n1954\t- **Oracle**: the pinned PHP 8.3.x CLI with the pinned ini. The comparison\n1955\t  covers stdout and the diagnostics stream, rendered in PHP's `Warning: ... in\n1956\t  <unit> on line N` form with the path replaced by the unit name and the line\n1957\t  derived from the side table (owner decision 2 of `yang.antlr.md` 8.5.1\n1958\t  reused), against PHP's stderr with paths normalized.\n1959\t- **Groups**: juggling (every pair of the comparison matrix, numeric strings,\n1960\t  arithmetic warnings and `TypeError`); arrays (key coercion, order, next\n1961\t  index after unset, copy-on-write observable through references, nested\n1962\t  writes, sort stability); references and lifecycle (section 4.6's three\n1963\t  sources); strings (bytes, interpolation forms, heredoc indentation,\n1964\t  `precision=14` output, `var_dump` text); functions (named arguments,"}
